window.docContents[65].resolve({"/Design-restrictions/Behaviour/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Behaviour":{"contents":"Each syntactic class has a relation and a function of the evaluator. The expressions have Eval and expr, the expressions that denote a location LEval and lval, and the commands Exec and cmd. The relation states the cases of its class with a result. The function covers every case of the same class, and its result is that of the relation or an error.The rules state each undefined case of C++ as a premise. The rule Div requires a divisor other than zero, and Arith and Div require a result in the range of int. The rule Delete requires an object at ℓ, and a virtual destructor when the static class differs from the class of the object. When a premise fails, the subject has no derivation. The function of the same class then gives the cause of error that names the premise, divisionByZero, overflow, doubleDelete or deleteWithoutVirtualDtor.The function returns into the monad CoreCpp.M, the exception transformer ExceptT Error over a state monad. The state holds only the trace of the derivation, not the store σ. Every handler of an exception in the evaluator records the trace and throws the exception again. So an error of a premise is the result of the conclusion. That monad states the propagation of error once, and no rule repeats it. The causes of error are the constructors of one type, so every case outside the rules has a name.Two kinds of cause stay outside the pattern. The causes undeclaredVariable, undeclaredFunction, arity, typeError and notCallable arise only in a program that skipped the type checker. The cause outOfFuel marks a derivation deeper than the fuel, and it is not a result of the semantics. The agreement between each relation and its function has no Lean proof, and issue #6 records the check.\n\nC++17 defines undefined behaviour as behaviour for which the standard imposes no requirements (N4659 §3.27). An expression whose result is not mathematically defined or not representable in its type has undefined behaviour (N4659 §8 ¶4), and so has a zero divisor (N4659 §8.6 ¶4). So does a delete through a pointer to a base without a virtual destructor (N4659 §8.3.5 ¶3). The end of the storage duration of an object makes every pointer to it invalid. Indirection through an invalid pointer and its deallocation by a second delete have undefined behaviour (N4659 §6.7 ¶4).Core C++ admits none of these cases. Each one fails a premise of every rule that could reach it, so the subject has no derivation. The evaluator reports error with the exit code 134, .The restriction gives every well typed program one of three outcomes, a value, error, or no termination. The semantics fixes the outcome, and no compiler chooses it. The examples of the repository show the difference, through bin/compare.| Example | g++ | Core C++ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| overflow.cpp | 0 | 134 |\n| division_by_zero.cpp | 0 | 134 |\n| out_of_bounds.cpp | 0 | 134 |\n| null_dereference.cpp | 139 | 134 |\n| double_delete.cpp | 133 | 134 |\n| delete_nonvirtual.cpp | 1 | 134 |A non terminating program has no derivation either. This is a limitation of the inductive big step style, not a restriction of the subset (Kahn, RR-0601, §4.4, p. 11).\n\nIn C++17 the evaluations of the operands of an operator are unsequenced, except where the standard notes otherwise (N4659 §4.6 ¶17). The initialisations of the parameters of a call are indeterminately sequenced with respect to each other (N4659 §8.2.2 ¶5). A side effect on a memory location that is unsequenced relative to another side effect or a value computation on that location has undefined behaviour (N4659 §4.6 ¶17).Core C++ evaluates left to right wherever C++17 leaves the order open, . It keeps the orders C++17 fixes, such as the right operand before the left one in an assignment (N4659 §8.18 ¶1).The restriction makes the order of the premises of each rule part of the rule. The store of one premise is the input of the next, so a construct has one applicable rule and one result, .\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Design restrictions","header":"9.1. Behaviour","id":"/Design-restrictions/Behaviour/#Core-C______--Design-restrictions--Behaviour"}});